---
title: "Elisabeth Diatta's beach-wrestling gold made history for Senegal"
summary: "At Dakar 2026, the 16-year-old wrestler won Senegal's first gold at an Olympic event, on home sand and in a sport with deep local roots."
category: Sport
date: 2026-11-13
author: "Fahamu News Desk"
---

When Elisabeth Diatta stepped onto the beach-wrestling mat in Saly, she was competing at a Youth Olympic Games hosted on her home continent. By the end of the 55-kilogram final on 7 November, the 16-year-old had made a second piece of history: she won Senegal's first gold medal at an Olympic event.

Diatta beat Japan's Reisa Kojima in the final. Her victory came during Dakar 2026, the first Olympic sporting event held on African soil. The Games ran across Dakar, Diamniadio and Saly, bringing together young athletes from around the world.

## A local sport on an international stage

Beach wrestling made its Olympic debut at the Youth Olympic Games. In Senegal, wrestling is more than a competition format: the local sport known as *laamb* is followed widely, and traditional wrestling has a long place in community life.

The Associated Press reported that Diatta comes from Casamance, where women have traditionally wrestled alongside men. Her win also drew attention to opportunities for girls in a sport whose professional scene is often dominated by men. Diatta's coach, Isabelle Sambu, is a two-time Olympian and nine-time African wrestling champion.

## What a first can mean

Dakar 2026 brought the Olympic movement to Africa for the first time. The event's significance is larger than one medal: Senegalese athletes, artists and organisers helped shape a global sports gathering on their own ground. Diatta's result gave that milestone a face, and connected a global competition to a sport familiar to many Senegalese fans.

